# No-Confidence Motion Against the Ministry: Debate of 24 August 1968
Tamil title: அமைச்சரவையின்மீது நம்பிக்கையில்லாத் தீர்மானம் - விவாதம்

Speech No. 51, 24 August 1968

The debate on the no-confidence motion against the Ministry began for its second day at about 10:30 a.m. on 24 August 1968 and continued until 2:00 p.m. The Honourable Deputy Speaker, Pulavar K. Govindan, presided.

At the beginning of the debate, DMK member K. M. Kathiravan delivered his maiden speech opposing the motion. He said the motion had been brought merely to accuse the ruling party and divert the people’s attention. A workers’ strike was an expression of the workers’ awakening, not a sign of administrative failure. He described the hardships workers had endured under Congress rule. He explained that unemployment had not been created by the DMK Government but had resulted from deficiencies in the three Five-Year Plans implemented during Congress rule.

Thiru P. G. Karuthiruman intervened. When the Chair intervened and asked that K. M. Kathiravan’s maiden speech be heard without interruption, members pointed to an interruption made by the Honourable Chief Minister the previous day. *[The concluding exchange on the first scan is damaged.]*

Thiru K. M. Kathiravan then resumed his speech and explained the shortcomings of the Five-Year Plans. He said the Tamil volunteer force differed from organisations such as the White Pigeon Force and was a shield, not a sword, for Tamils. Speaking about prohibition, he used statistics to explain that the DMK Government was more firmly committed to prohibition than other State Governments. He concluded by showing that all the accusations in the motion lacked substance.

Congress member Thiru D. Martin next spoke in support of the motion. He described DMK rule as dictatorial. Speaking about the fire incidents, he alleged that the fires were a conspiracy by the ruling party and that its members had set huts ablaze so they could replace the burnt dwellings and gain a good name. As evidence, he cited the line “The huts will be burnt” from the film *Nadodi Mannan*.

**The Honourable Thiru M. Karunanidhi:** In the film *Nadodi Mannan*, did it say that the huts must be set on fire as soon as the word “hut” occurred...?

**Thiru D. Martin:** They will be burnt...

**The Honourable Thiru M. Karunanidhi:** He says the line was “They will be burnt.” The person who wrote that dialogue is now the commander of their own party.

**Thiru D. Martin:** In which party is he? This arose suddenly under a Government that came suddenly...

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** By referring to the story of *Nadodi Mannan*, in which a wanderer became a king, and saying that wanderers have come to rule the country, if they think the nation will therefore accept that they descend from a royal line, I consider them mistaken. Even in that film, he did not come to rule merely because he had been a wanderer. The two men in it had the same appearance, and that is how he came to rule. He did not come to rule because he was a wanderer. It is not enough merely to watch a film; one must understand it.

**Thiru D. Martin:** I speak only after understanding it. I say this knowing that the Constitution gives everyone the opportunity to govern.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** When that is so, I wish to know whether it seems courteous to point to what occurred in the film and say that this is what happens when people who were wanderers come to rule.

**Thiru D. Martin:** When the Chief Minister tells us what an old woman supposedly said about the fire, why is there no room for me to mention what appeared in a film?

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** What I wish to ask is simply this: by pointing to a film in which a wanderer came to rule, and speaking generally while keeping us in mind as people who were wanderers and have now come to rule, is that courteous speech?

**Thiru D. Martin:** My purpose in mentioning a scene from the film in this House was that the scene’s important point was this: as soon as the wanderer became king... he said the huts would be burnt...

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** *[Part of the reply across the page boundary is damaged.]* I do not wish to pursue the film. I ask whether it is right to speak while keeping us in mind.

**Thiru D. Martin:** I did not call the Chief Minister a wanderer. Such a scene appeared in *Nadodi Mannan*...

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Look again at what you said when you have time. There is honour in people who were wanderers coming to rule. There is no honour in those who once ruled becoming wanderers.

**Thiru D. Martin:** I wish to remind you that they may return to rule.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** If Venus enters a favourable phase, that may happen. Our coming to rule is a matter of honour, not disgrace. One should not speak discourteously in the House. It is certainly necessary for Thiru Martin to distinguish himself as a legislative speaker. But he cannot do so through the methods he is now using. If he changes those methods, he can shine whichever side he sits on. This method offers only petty satisfaction. When he goes outside after speaking, someone may pat him on the back, and he may feel pleased that he said, “Did you see? I called him a wanderer.” But that satisfaction will not last.

As Thiru D. Martin continued, he alleged that preferential appointments were being given to relatives of DMK ministers. He cited the appointment of the head of the Chennai Sangeetha Nataka Akademi as an example.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I wish to know whether there is a law saying that, even if ministers’ relatives possess the qualifications for a post, they must not be appointed merely because they are related to ministers.

**Thiru D. Martin:** When Carnatic musicians more accomplished than he—Semmangudi Srinivasa Iyer, Chittoor Subramaniam, Sattur Subramania Iyer, Madurai Somu, M. L. Vasanthakumari and M. S. Subbulakshmi—are available, what reason other than his being the minister’s relative can explain giving that post to Chidambaram Jayaraman?

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I had thought only that my friend did not know how to watch and understand a film. I regret that he does not even know the standing of musicians. Every person he mentioned would acknowledge that Chidambaram Jayaraman is an excellent musician. My friend should ask those trained in music and learn that Jayaraman’s standing is of the first rank. He also listed some people and asked why one of them could not have been appointed. Some of those very people would themselves appear on the Public Works Minister’s list of relatives.

Thiru D. Martin next alleged that members of the ruling party interfered in police administration and cited several incidents.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** When he says “members of the Kazhagam,” one of the two incidents cited concerns Kaduvetti Thiagarajan. A case was registered against him, he was tried and punished, and he was also expelled from the Pudukkottai unit of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam with which he was associated. *[The remainder of this intervention is damaged.]*

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** It would be proper for my friend to say whether he accepts what I have stated before proceeding further.

**Thiru D. Martin:** When a member of the Legislature speaks in this House, it is his choice to say what he wishes. It is not proper for him to alter his view merely so that someone else may accept it.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** When he supplies incorrect information and ministers offer an explanation, he should say whether he accepts it. I did not say that he must accept it. He may say that he does not. It is not right to say neither and simply stop at that point and proceed.

**Thiru D. Martin:** I mentioned those two incidents to say that Kazhagam members interfere in police administration. I have a right to say so.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I stated that the person involved was prosecuted and punished and was removed from the Kazhagam. I did not say he must accept the explanation. If he says that he does not, people reading the record can place the Chief Minister’s explanation beside the member’s statement and understand the situation.

**Thiru D. Martin:** At Tiruchendur...

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** My friend practises as an advocate. I wish him much success in court. But if, in court, he raises a matter and then proceeds without addressing the defect pointed out by the opposing side, people will conclude that he moved on because he had no case.

**Thiru D. Martin:** After arguments are made for the plaintiff and defendant in court, it is the judge who gives judgment. I speak for the opposition. The Chief Minister has presented his party’s case. The public will give judgment.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Since the public will give judgment, say that you do not accept my explanation. Even that would be satisfactory. If you proceed as though nothing happened, the public will repeatedly note that the Chief Minister offered an explanation and that, even afterward, the member continued without saying whether he rejected or accepted it. They may wonder whether the Chief Minister’s explanation bewildered or unsettled him.

Thiru D. Martin said he would leave the matter to public judgment and continued. He alleged that ruling-party members protected a person who showed a black flag in Tiruchendur and interfered with the police. The Chair asked him to bring his speech to a close.

The Honourable Deputy Leader Thiru V. R. Nedunchezhiyan then continued. Thiru R. Ilamvazhuthi spoke. Thiru R. Rathina Thevar of the Swatantra Party, an allied party of the DMK, began by trying to explain the 1957 communal violence at Mudukulathur. Thiru K. Vinayakam intervened.

## Recovered Source English: Image 8 Exchange

**Thiru K. Vinayakam:** Sir, on a point of order. The subject before this House is the no-confidence motion tabled by the Honourable Leader of the Opposition against the present Ministry. So any speech that is made on the floor of the House should pertain to this Ministry and its activities, not to the days of Adam and Eve. The Honourable Member should not waste the time of the House over all those things, and I am worried about the time factor. If we are going to sit for twenty days, we will talk about those things.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I am glad that the Honourable Member wants us to be quite logical in our approach to this problem. How did he accommodate the Leader of the Opposition yesterday when he talked about the Neyveli Project and the Perambur Coach Factory and also other things which have no bearing at all on the no-confidence motion?

**Thiru K. Vinayakam:** You should have questioned him at that time.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I might not have questioned him, but the Honourable Member should have raised objections at that time itself, as he is doing now with regard to the other Honourable Member, saying that it is irrelevant. But the Honourable Member kept quiet at that time.

**Thiru K. Vinayakam:** It pays me.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** So your logic is what pays you. It is very bad logic.

## Tamil Translation of the Recovered English Exchange

**திரு. கே. விநாயகம்:** ஐயா, ஒழுங்குப் பிரச்சினை ஒன்றை எழுப்புகிறேன். இன்றைய அமைச்சரவைக்கு எதிராக மாண்புமிகு எதிர்க்கட்சித் தலைவர் கொண்டுவந்த நம்பிக்கையில்லாத் தீர்மானமே இந்த அவையின் முன் உள்ளது. எனவே அவையில் ஆற்றப்படும் எந்த உரையும் இந்த அமைச்சரவையையும் அதன் செயல்களையும் பற்றியதாக இருக்க வேண்டும்; ஆதாம் ஏவாள் காலத்தைப் பற்றியதாக இருக்கக் கூடாது. அந்த விஷயங்களைக் கூறி மாண்புமிகு உறுப்பினர் அவையின் நேரத்தை வீணாக்கக் கூடாது. நேரத்தைப் பற்றியே நான் கவலைப்படுகிறேன். இருபது நாட்கள் அமரப் போகிறோம் என்றால் அவற்றைப் பேசலாம்.

**மாண்புமிகு திரு. சி. என். அண்ணாதுரை:** இந்தப் பிரச்சினையை நாம் முற்றிலும் தர்க்கபூர்வமாக அணுக வேண்டும் என்று மாண்புமிகு உறுப்பினர் விரும்புவதில் மகிழ்ச்சி அடைகிறேன். நம்பிக்கையில்லாத் தீர்மானத்தோடு எந்தத் தொடர்பும் இல்லாத நெய்வேலித் திட்டம், பெரம்பூர் இரயில் பெட்டித் தொழிற்சாலை மற்றும் பிற விஷயங்களை எதிர்க்கட்சித் தலைவர் நேற்று பேசியபோது அவருக்கு இவர் எவ்வாறு இடமளித்தார்?

**திரு. கே. விநாயகம்:** அப்போதே நீங்கள் அவரைக் கேள்வி கேட்டிருக்க வேண்டும்.

**மாண்புமிகு திரு. சி. என். அண்ணாதுரை:** நான் அவரைக் கேள்வி கேட்காமல் இருந்திருக்கலாம். ஆனால் இப்போது மற்ற மாண்புமிகு உறுப்பினரின் பேச்சு தொடர்பற்றது என்று எதிர்ப்பது போலவே அப்போதும் இவர் எதிர்ப்புத் தெரிவித்திருக்க வேண்டும். ஆனால் அப்போது மாண்புமிகு உறுப்பினர் அமைதியாக இருந்தார்.

**திரு. கே. விநாயகம்:** அது எனக்குப் பயனளிக்கிறது.

**மாண்புமிகு திரு. சி. என். அண்ணாதுரை:** அப்படியானால் உங்களுக்குப் பயனளிப்பதுதான் உங்கள் தர்க்கம். அது மிகவும் மோசமான தர்க்கம்.

Thiru R. Rathina Thevar continued his speech. He referred to actions taken during Congress rule by Thiru Kamaraj and Thiru C. Subramaniam. Thiru P. G. Karuthiruman intervened, saying that it was not right to speak by naming Kamaraj and Subramaniam in that manner.

**The Honourable Deputy Speaker:** They mention them because they were ministers in this House.

**The Honourable Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** It would be appropriate to refer to “this Government.” There is no need to name the individuals who administered it.

The debate continued. After several members had spoken, the Deputy Speaker brought the day’s sitting to a close at about 2:00 p.m.

---NOTES---
The source is a summarized Assembly record containing editorial narration, quoted exchanges and several visibly truncated passages rather than a continuous verbatim speech. Every available exchange is translated; damaged boundaries on Images 1, 3, 5 and 7 are marked rather than reconstructed. Image 8 is an English-language printed passage that was processed with the Tamil OCR model; it was recovered from the original scan with English OCR and is retained in both recovered English and Tamil translation blocks. “Sukkira dasai” is an astrological idiom for a fortunate period under Venus. Historical spellings and party labels are retained.
